Resource List
	The resources of the selected widget and their values are
	listed here.
	
	You can control the way resources are listed with the `Sort'
	button (see Help/Resource Sort).
	
	You can use a `pretty' resource list which displays resources
	with pretty fonts and icons, or a `fast' resource list which
	uses fixed width fonts and characters instead of icons.
	See `Help/On List Icons...' for an explanation on the various
	resource icons.
	
	Selecting a line in the list moves a text field on top of the
	value. You can edit the resource value in this text field, or
	use the `...' button on the right (or double-click in the
	resource name) to pop an appropriate resource
	editor (see Help/On Specialized Resource Editors).
	
	Typing Return in the text field of the current resource activates
	the `Set Values' button, except if the value is empty in which
	case the `Rebuild' button is activated.
	
Apply Options
	This box lets you choose how the resources set for the widget
	in the resource list will be applied.
	
	
	- `Apply' lets you select which resources will be applied:
	
	--- `all resources' applies all set resources (i.e. resources
	for which a value was specified by the user, not only
	resources that have been modified since the last apply),
	
	--- `selected resources' applies only resources that are
	selected in the resource list;
	
	- `by' lets you select how the resources will be applied:
	
	--- `re-creating widgets' destroys and re-creates widgets
	with the new resource values,
	
	--- `setting values' calls XtSetValues to apply new resource
	values;
	
	- `to' selects which widgets the resources will be applied
	to:
	
	--- `self' applies only to the widget edited in this
	resource window,
	
	--- `selected widgets' applies to all currently selected
	widgets,
	
	--- `selected sub-tree' applies to the currently selected
	widget and all its descendants.
	
	
	Once you have selected the desired options, click `OK'
	to apply the resources. Click `Cancel' to unmap the
	window without applying resources.
	
	NOTE: Clicking the `Rebuild' button is equivalent to 
	clicking `...' and clicking `OK'. Clicking the `Set Values'
	button is equivalent to clicking `...', selecting
	`setting values' and clicking `OK'.

Resource Sort
	The `Sort' button displays a sub-window with which you can
	control the way resources are displayed in the resource list.
	
	
	The `Preferred Resources' area is a list of resources that
	will be displayed at the top of the resource list, You can enter
	either resource names or resource types (e.g. XmString). If
	the `Show only preferred resources' toggle is set, only these
	resources will be displayed in the list.
	
	
	The `Sort Order' area lets you choose the order in which
	resources are sorted:
	
	- `alphabetical' sorts resources in alphabetical order,
	
	- `by subclass' sorts resources inherited from superclasses
	first, starting from Core down to the widget's class.
	
	- `by superclass' sorts resources defined by subclasses first,
	starting from the widget's class down to Core.
	
	
	The next toggles let you place XFaceMaker resources (i.e. the
	xfmCreateCallback) at the top or at the bottom of the list.
	
	Similarly, you can place XFaceMaker active values at the top
	or at the bottom.
	
	
	If `Gather set resources' is checked, the resources that you
	modified will be placed at the top of the list.
	
	If `Show template resources' is checked, resources inherited
	from templates (and not modified) will be displayed, otherwise
	they will be hidden.
	
	If `Show restricted resources' is checked, resources that are
	not valid or that you may not modify using XFaceMaker will
	be displayed, otherwise they will be hidden.
	
	
	The `OK' button applies the changes you have made to this
	resource list.
	
	The `Cancel' button goes back to the list
	without changing the sorting policy.
	
	The `Set Default' button makes the choices currently displayed
	the default sort options for other resource windows that you
	will open later.
	
	The `Help' button displays this text.
	
	
	The resource list sorting options are saved to the .xfm.startup
	file when you use the `File/Save Prefs' command.

Specialized Resource Editors
	
	XFaceMaker provides specialized dialog boxes to edit
	many types of widget resources more efficiently.
	Standard specialized editors include a color editor,
	a font editor, a translation table editor, an enumeration
	editor, and many others. Specialized editors can be
	defined by the user (as a .fm file) and added to a
	customized version of XFaceMaker using the FmAddResourceType
	function (see the documentation).
	
	Specialized editors are displayed by clicking in the `...'
	button at the right of the current resource, or by
	double-clicking in the resource name.
	
	Once an editor is popped, you can edit the value using
	the appropriate controls. For details on a particular
	editor, click in the `Help' button when this editor is
	mapped.
	
	Once you are done editing the resource value, click `OK'
	to apply the resource change. The `OK' button records the
	new resource value, unmaps the editor (i.e. goes back to
	the resource list) and rebuilds the widget.
	
	You can also use the `Rebuild' and `Set Values' buttons,
	which have the same effect as when the resource list is
	displayed (see `Help/On Window...'). These buttons do
	not unmap the editor (i.e. do no go back to the list):
	they are useful to see the effect of a resource change
	while continuing to edit the resource.
	
	The `Set' button records the new resource value in the
	resource list and unmaps the editor, but does not apply
	the change to the widget: you must do a `Rebuild' or a
	`Set Values' later to apply changes. This is useful to
	set several resources which must be applied simultaneoulsy.
	
	The `Cancel' button unmaps the editor and goes back to the
	list without changing any resource value.
	
	The `Help' button displays help on the editor currently
	displayed.
	
	Additional buttons may appear depending on the editor
	displayed. For example, for String resources, a `GLS'
	button lets you add the XFaceMaker internationalization
	prefix to the string.

Textual Resource Editor
	The textual Resource Editor lets you examine and edit
	the resources of the widget in a purely textual form.
	To map the textual Editor, click in the `Edit' button
	at the bottom of the resource window. Afterwards, click
	in `List' to go back to the resource list.
	
	The textual resource editor is useful to see
	a summary of the resources set so far. It can also be
	used by experimented users to quickly edit
	resource values.
	
	In this editor, each resource is specified with the format
	`name = value'. If `value' is a multi-line value, line
	breaks must be escaped by backslash (\) characters, or
	the first character of the next line must be a Tab. In
	other words, values end just before the first newline
	character not followed by a Tab and not preceeded by a
	backslash.
	
	The `Custom Editor' button above the text lets you call
	your favorite text editor to modify the resources. The
	resources will be transferred to the editor, where they
	can be edited. Every time you save your work, the resources
	are transferred back to the XFaceMaker window. You can
	choose the editor you want to use by setting the
	FMEDITOR environment variable using the Environment box
	of XFaceMaker. For example, set FMEDITOR to `vi' to use
	vi, or to `wx242' to use NSL's X11 editor Wx 2.4.2 or
	later.
	
	The use of this editor is not recommended to new users
	because it
	is easy to make mistakes while typing multi-line resources.
	If you forget a continuation character, the beginning
	of the next line will be considered as a new resource
	and will be listed as a `non-existing' resource in the
	resource list. If you think part of your resource has
	disappeared, make sure the `Show restricted resources'
	toggle is set so that erroneous resources are not hidden
	by XFaceMaker.
	
Attachment Resource Editor
	The Attachment Editor helps you set the attachment resources for
	the children of a Form widget. It can be popped by clicking on
	the `...' button in any of the attachment contraint resources
	for a child of a Form widget. It can also be popped directly
	using the command `Edit/Attachments'.
	
	There are basically 2 ways you can use this editor:
	
	1. You can use the arrows on the left of the editor. To use the arrows,
	do the following:
	
	- set the `position' toggle if you want the widget to be attached by position,
	otherwise set any other toggle in the same radio box;
	
	- unset the `no offset' toggle if you want XFaceMaker to automatically set
	the offset so that the widget stays at its present position, otherwise set it;
	
	- click on the arrow corresponding to the direction for which you want to define
	the attachment;
	
	- if you have set the `position' toggle, XFaceMaker attaches the widget to its
	present position; otherwise, the cursor changes to a question arrow, and you can
	select the widget to which you want to attach the current widget (thsi can be either
	the parent Form an a sibling of the widget): XFaceMaker sets the attachment resources
	according to your choice.
	
	2. You can use only the buttons and fields on the left, and then click on `Apply'
	at the bottom of the editor to apply your choices. When appropriate, the `offset'
	and `position' fields are automatically updated according to the current widget
	position.
	
	The button at the middle of the arrow pad can be used to select another direction
	for which you want to edit attachments: click on the button, then on the arrow
	corresponding to the new direction.

String Resource Editor
	The specialized String resource editor lets you edit resources of type String, or
	resources for which there is no special editor. It is
	also popped to edit Callback resources: in that case,
	you can type a FACE script which will be called by the
	callback.
	
	You can type multi-line values without escaping line
	breaks: this will be done automatically by XFaceMaker.
	
	The `Custom Editor' button above the text lets you call
	your favorite text editor to modify the resources. The
	resources will be transferred to the editor, where they
	can be edited. Every time you save your work, the resources
	are transferred back to the XFaceMaker window. You can
	choose the editor you want to use by setting the
	FMEDITOR environment variable using the Environment box
	of XFaceMaker. For example, set FMEDITOR to `vi' to use
	vi, or to `wx242' to use NSL's X11 editor Wx 2.4.2 or
	later.
	
	If you are editing a callback, a `Select' button appears
	at the bottom of the window. When clicked, it allows you
	to select a widget in your interface, in the widget tree
	or in the widget list. The path of the selected widget
	will be inserted in the text.
	
	A `GLS'	button lets you add the XFaceMaker internationalization
	prefix to the string.
	
	For a general help on how to use specialized resource
	editors, go back to the resource list (e.g. click `Cancel')
	and see `Help/On Specialized Editors'.
	
Color Resource Editor
	The specialized Colors resource editor lets you edit resources of type Pixel.
	
	On black and white screens, you can choose either
	`Black' or `White' using toggles.
	
	On color screens, you can choose a color from the palette
	by clicking on
	a small square, or click the `RGB...' button and define
	your own color using either Red/Green/Blue or
	Hue/Saturation/Value components.
	
	The palette displays the colors contained in the file
	`.xfm.colors' in XFaceMaker's library directory,
	in your home directory or in the current directory. You
	can change the colors you want displayed in the palette
	by editing the file. Note that the colors displayed in
	the palette are approximated using a dithering algorithm,
	so as not to use too many colormap entries. To see the
	actual color, select the square in the palette and click
	the `RGB' button.
	
	If you define a new color, XFaceMaker will ask you if
	you want to save it to a .xfm.colors file. This file
	will be read the next time you launch XFaceMaker, and
	the new color will be added to the palette automatically.
	
	For a general help on how to use specialized resource
	editors, go back to the resource list (e.g. click `Cancel')
	and see `Help/On Specialized Editors'.
	
Integer Resource Editor
	The specialized Integer resource editor lets you edit integer resources.
	
	Use the scrollbar to define the value. The minimum
	and maximum values can be changed by clicking in the
	buttons in which they are displayed: the left mouse
	button divides the value by ten, the right mouse button
	multiplies the value by ten.
	
	For a general help on how to use specialized resource
	editors, go back to the resource list (e.g. click `Cancel')
	and see `Help/On Specialized Editors'.

Boolean Resource Editor
	The specialized Boolean resource editor lets you edit boolean resources.
	
	Click `True' or `False'.
	
	For a general help on how to use specialized resource
	editors, go back to the resource list (e.g. click `Cancel')
	and see `Help/On Specialized Editors'.

Enumeration Resource Editor
	The specialized Enumeration resource editor lets you edit enumerated resources, i.e
	resources accepting a fixed set of scalar values.
	
	The choices are displayed in a list, with the current
	setting highlighted. You can select another choice and
	click `Rebuild' or `Set Values'. You can also double-click
	on a choice, which activates the `Rebuild' button.
	
	For a general help on how to use specialized resource
	editors, go back to the resource list (e.g. click `Cancel')
	and see `Help/On Specialized Editors'.

Font Resource Editor
	The specialized Font resource editor lets you edit font resources.
	
	The font editor lets you select a font name conforming
	to the X Logical Font Description format by choosing
	the font family, weight, style, and size. When you select
	a font family, the weight, style and size menus are
	filled automatically with the available choices for that
	family. Note that the foundry names are listed together
	with the family names.
	
	You can also choose a font name not conforming to the
	XLFD format using the `Miscellaneous Fonts' list on the
	right.
	
	An example of the currently selected font is displayed
	in a text field at the bottom of the editor. If you
	selected an invalid combination, a warning message is
	displayed instead of the font sample.
	
	For a general help on how to use specialized resource
	editors, go back to the resource list (e.g. click `Cancel')
	and see `Help/On Specialized Editors'.

Image Resource Editor
	The specialized Image resource editor lets you edit Pixmap and Bitmap resources.
	
	You can select an image in one of three ways:
	
	- You can select a predefined bitmap from the scrolled
	area on the right, which displays bitmaps contained in
	the directory /usr/include/X11/bitmaps.
	
	- You can load a file using the `Open...' button.
	You can select either a bitmap (.bm) file, or a color
	Pixmap file in the X Window Dump (XWD) format.
	
	- You can draw an image yourself using the ImageEdit
	widget, which lets you draw in free-hand mode or define
	simple geometric objects. You can select the current color
	with an option menu.
	
	In all cases, the selected image is displayed in the
	ImageEdit widget and also in a sample field at the bottom
	right of the editor.
	
	You can save modified images using the `Save...' button.
	The image will be saved in bitmap format if it contains
	only two colors. Otherwise, it will be saved in XWD format.
	
	The `New' button creates a new blank image that you can
	edit. You must enter the size of the image in the `width'
	and `height' fields before using the `New' button.
	
	For a general help on how to use specialized resource
	editors, go back to the resource list (e.g. click `Cancel')
	and see `Help/On Specialized Editors'.

Translations Resource Editor
	The specialized Translations resource editor lets you edit translation tables.
	
	The `Translation Table' list displays the translations
	currently defined. Note that the X Toolkit provides no
	mechanism for printing an existing translation table, so
	XFaceMaker cannot display the default value of a translation
	table, but only a value that has been defined previously
	using XFaceMaker.
	
	To edit a particular translation, select a line in the
	list: the translation is displayed in the `Translation'
	text field. You can edit it manually, or use the `Event
	type' and `Actions' lists at the bottom to select another
	event type or action. If you select the `Eval...' action,
	a text editor pops up and lets you enter a FACE script
	that will be executed instead of a predefined action.
	The `eval' action lets you define complex actions inside
	XFaceMaker. The event parameter passed to the action
	is available through the `$' parameter of the FACE script.
	
	You can `show' the details of the event to XFaceMaker
	using the keyboard/mouse icon. For example, to specify
	the event `Ctrl<ButtonPress>Button2':
	
	- select `ButtonPress' in the `Event type' list,
	
	- click the mouse button 2 in the keyboard/mouse icon while
	holding the Control key: the desired event is replaced
	in the edited translation.
	
	To specify a key press event, you must first move the
	keyboard focus to the keyboard/mouse icon, by clicking
	in it for instance.
	
	The `Add' button adds the translation to the table. If the
	same event if already present, the translation is replaced.
	
	The `Replace' button replaces the currently selected translation
	by the edited translation.
	
	The `Delete' button deletes the selected translation.
	
	The `override', `augment' and `replace' toggles insert
	the `#override', `#augment' or `#replace' keyword
	respectively at the beginning of the translation table.
	
	For a general help on how to use specialized resource
	editors, go back to the resource list (e.g. click `Cancel')
	and see `Help/On Specialized Editors'.
	
Float Resource Editor
	The specialized Float resource editor lets you edit
	floating-point resources.
	
	A scale widget lets you define the value between a
	minimum and maximum value. The minimum and maximum can
	be changed using the arrows at each side of the text
	field. The left arrow divides the value by 10, the right
	arrow multiplies the value by 10.
	
	For a general help on how to use specialized resource
	editors, go back to the resource list (e.g. click `Cancel')
	and see `Help/On Specialized Editors'.

Table Resource Editor
	The specialized Table resource editor lets you edit resources which accept arrays
	of values, for example the items of a list.
	
	The list displays the elements of the list. Select
	an element of the list to edit it in the text field
	below. You can also type a new value to add it to the
	list.
	
	If a specialized editor is available for the list items,
	a `...' button appears at the right of the text field.
	Clicking in this `...' button pops the specialized editor.
	Clicking `OK' in the editor inserts the value in the
	table editor's text field.
	
	For a general help on how to use specialized resource
	editors, go back to the resource list (e.g. click `Cancel')
	and see `Help/On Specialized Editors'.

Widget Resource Editor
	The specialized Widget resource editor lets you edit resources pointing to other
	widgets.
	
	You can type the path of the widget using the FACE script
	syntax (e.g. parent.filePulldown), or simply the name
	of a widget which is then assumed to be a sibling widget,
	for example `filePulldown'.
	
	The `Select' button lets you select a widget in the interface
	or in the widget tree with the mouse.
	
	For a general help on how to use specialized resource
	editors, go back to the resource list (e.g. click `Cancel')
	and see `Help/On Specialized Editors'.
